      "math_academy_id": "e-8440",
      "problem": "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle \\int 4\\sinh 2x \\cosh 2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin {2\\theta}=2\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh {2\\theta} =2\\sinh \\theta \\cosh \\theta\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta= 2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\sinh {4x} =2\\sinh 2x \\cosh 2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sinh {4x}= \\sinh 2x \\cosh 2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
